ng message gains traction with independents, this could tighten toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chris Pappas (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Hampshire's 1st District is an open seat after Chris Pappas left it to run for Senate, and Democrats have turned to Stefany Shaheen, a first-time candidate from the state's best-known political family. Republican Anthony DiLorenzo has sought to set himself apart from Trump on tariffs and tone in a district that has historically swung between the parties.\n\nThe evidence points to a Democratic edge. Nine race raters place the seat at Lean D to Safe D, with most calling it Likely D. The only public poll, from the University of New Hampshire in late September, showed Shaheen ahead 46-42 among likely voters. Betting markets put her win chances above 90 percent. Fundraising is nearly even, though Shaheen ends with more cash and super PAC backing. A strong Democratic environment adds to her advantage, but the narrow poll and the district's swing history keep this short of safe.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.937, "margin": 10.01}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9238, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9245, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8928, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8695, "margin": 7.5}]}